metal2vulkan: a copied no-copy buffer gives back only what the GPU changed (Scion)

Upstream:  metal2vulkan, https://github.com/steelbrain/metal2vulkan, at 720ef1ce9ae2a2068999a8675717418b9416283e, after
           AdrenoMetal's 16 patches to it and Scion's 0001 to 0005; tools/metal/mk-metal2vulkan.sh applies them in that order,
           then this.
Licence:   GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later, metal2vulkan's (its LICENSE); this patch is a change to it and under the same
           terms. Nothing of it is in Scion's MIT or APSL files.
Author:    the HakiOS authors (Scion), 14 November 2026.

Why. The window server on Metal (build 52, camtl=1) died of SIGSEGV within a minute, twice, in memmove from
Buffer::copy_host_bytes (host_sync::copy_out, on the command buffer's Transition thread), at a page boundary of the caller's
bytes. 0002 copies a no-copy buffer the device could not import back into the caller's bytes as a command buffer that writes
it completes, and one its commands use untracked counts as written. The copy back took every 4 KiB where the buffer's own
memory and the caller's bytes differed, which is also where the caller changed its bytes after the copy in. CoreAnimation's
buffers over memory it maps read-only were written there, a fault. And on writable memory the caller's newer writes would have
been overwritten with the old copy.

What. CopiedHost keeps a 64-bit digest of each 4 KiB of the buffer's own memory as it last agreed with the caller's bytes,
recorded at each copy in. A copy out gives back only the chunks whose digest changed since, which are the chunks the GPU wrote,
and records the new digest. A chunk the GPU did not write is left as the caller has it, read-only or rewritten. A buffer never
copied in gives nothing back. The comparison of a copy in is unchanged; a copy out no longer reads the caller's bytes.

--- a/crates/metal2vulkan-runtime/src/buffer.rs
+++ b/crates/metal2vulkan-runtime/src/buffer.rs
@@ -91,6 +91,26 @@
     pointer: NonNull<u8>,
     length: u64,
     deallocator: Mutex<Option<Deallocator>>,
+    /// A digest of each 4 KiB of the buffer's own memory as it last agreed with the caller's
+    /// bytes (after a copy in or out): a copy out takes back only what the GPU changed since, so a
+    /// buffer used untracked (its writes not known) gives back nothing it did not write, and the
+    /// caller's later writes, or bytes it maps read-only, are left alone.
+    synced: Mutex<Vec<u64>>,
+}
+
+/// A 64-bit digest of `bytes` (FxHash's step over 8-byte words): whether a 4 KiB chunk of a copied
+/// no-copy buffer changed since it was last synced.
+fn chunk_digest(bytes: &[u8]) -> u64 {
+    let mut hash = bytes.len() as u64;
+    let mut words = bytes.chunks_exact(8);
+    for word in &mut words {
+        let w = u64::from_le_bytes(word.try_into().unwrap_or([0; 8]));
+        hash = (hash.rotate_left(5) ^ w).wrapping_mul(0x9e37_79b9_7f4a_7c15);
+    }
+    for &b in words.remainder() {
+        hash = (hash.rotate_left(5) ^ u64::from(b)).wrapping_mul(0x9e37_79b9_7f4a_7c15);
+    }
+    hash
 }
 
